Step 7 — Model training
Use XGBoost or LightGBM (handles feature interactions, gives feature importance)
Time-based split — train on earlier weeks, test on most recent weeks (never random split, that leaks future data)
Output: P(will_trend) — a probability per topic per week
Step 8 — Prediction

Take the current week's row (where will_trend is unknown), run it through the trained model → get a ranked probability list of topics likely to trend in the next 2 weeks.




Algorithms:
Both XGBoost (eXtreme Gradient Boosting) and LightGBM (Light Gradient Boosting Machine) are implementations of the same core idea: build many small decision trees, one after another, where each new tree tries to fix the mistakes of the previous ones.

How gradient boosting actually works (conceptually)

Think of it like a chain of specialists, not one genius:

Tree 1 looks at all your topics' data (SMPR, momentum, ratio_to_peak, etc.) and makes a rough first guess at will_trend for each row. It'll get plenty wrong.
Tree 2 doesn't start over — it specifically looks at where Tree 1 was wrong and tries to correct those errors.
Tree 3 corrects what Tree 1 + Tree 2 still got wrong.
This repeats for hundreds of trees, each one nudging the overall prediction a little closer to correct.
Final prediction = combined output of all trees added together (weighted).